1. Full texts not accessed, so equations or claims are unconfirmed for:
   - Collet et al. 2014 (abstract only);
   - Tiruta-Barna et al. 2016 (abstract only; model equations seen only in the Pigné 2020 ESM restatement);
   - Cardellini et al. 2018 Temporalis (abstract and software docs only);
   - Collinge et al. 2013 (equation only via Müller 2025);
   - Heijungs & Suh 2002 and Suh & Heijungs 2007 (only via ESPA and the Pinsonnault thesis);
   - Sporchia et al. 2026 (abstract summary only; their extended matrix notation should be checked before fixing the chapter's notation);
   - Beloin-Saint-Pierre et al. 2017 J Ind Ecol (abstract only);
   - the published version of Pinsonnault et al. 2014 (equations read in the thesis manuscript version);
   - ESPA's ESM 1, which defines the convolution product.
2. The candidate 'Beloin-Saint-Pierre et al. 2017, Journal of Cleaner Production, A new method for addressing temporal...' was not found. The 2017 follow-up is in J Ind Ecol 21(5):1128-1138. Treat the J Clean Prod reference as unconfirmed or nonexistent.
3. ESPA lists 'N(I-T) < 1' as a convergence condition. This looks inconsistent with the spectral condition on T; it may be a typo. Unconfirmed.
4. No primary LCA source writes a continuous two-time technosphere kernel h(t, s), or a Laplace or z-transform of the convolution-Leontief series. The transform-domain statements and the block-Toeplitz characterisation in the synthesis are my derivations and need their own proof or citation in the chapter.
5. The end-of-life residual anchored to the study end T (rather than to a component's time zero) is not treated in any source found. It is a boundary or windowing term, not a fixed-kernel convolution. How to make it consistent with T-dependent cumulative accounting is open.
6. For building components, which representation is right is unresolved in the literature: convolution (dedicated production per renewal call) or DyPLCA-style scheduling with capacity factors (shared infrastructure, batch supply). Pigné et al. criticise convolution but give no quantitative comparison.
7. Time-varying LCIA kernels (e.g. GWP impulse responses under changing background concentrations) are mentioned by Müller et al. 2025 as needed but not implemented. That would make the impact-side kernel two-time as well.
